1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,785
This is a separate concept for the agentic future of SalesPlay.

2
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:10,800
The seller is Jamie at Aster Automation, selling machines that assemble
laminated transformer

3
00:00:11,170 --> 00:00:16,250
cores. Jamie’s goal is to find manufacturers where that equipment could
address an in-house

4
00:00:16,890 --> 00:00:20,850
assembly need. The outcome is a useful next conversation.

5
00:00:21,170 --> 00:00:26,240
SalesPlay takes responsibility for the investigation, while Jamie
supplies business context and

6
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:32,800
new customer facts. The accounts and agent actions in this local preview
are simulated.

7
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:37,370
Start with the growth goal, the product, and North America as the
territory.

8
00:00:37,970 --> 00:00:39,630
Then start the investigation.

9
00:00:41,525 --> 00:00:43,840
SalesPlay now works through a visible plan.

10
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:50,200
It establishes the market reason, narrows the relevant manufacturing
process, investigates

11
00:00:50,570 --> 00:00:55,280
accounts beyond the customer list, checks the fit, and prepares a
decision.

12
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:58,530
Each completed action links to the records it used.

13
00:00:58,620 --> 00:01:02,460
The Department of Energy provides public industry context.

14
00:01:02,460 --> 00:01:08,160
A simulated specialist segment map connects that context to laminated-
core assembly.

15
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:11,680
Fictional account profiles supply possible matches.

16
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:16,160
This activity log shows observable actions and their outputs.

17
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:19,520
In a live product, research tools would perform those steps.

18
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:24,410
Here, a deterministic scenario makes the proposed behavior inspectable
and repeatable.

19
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:31,430
The first investigation surfaces Ridgeway, an account outside Jamie’s
existing list.

20
00:01:31,735 --> 00:01:34,920
HarborGrid is a known account with an expansion review.

21
00:01:35,220 --> 00:01:39,520
Summit is excluded because its wound-core process does not fit the
equipment.

22
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:41,800
Open Ridgeway to see the reasoning.

23
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:45,360
Market demand explains why the industry is worth investigating.

24
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:48,440
The segment map narrows the relevant process.

25
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:51,690
The starting profile suggests in-house assembly.

26
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:56,880
That last claim is provisional, and it matters most to the next
decision.

27
00:01:57,220 --> 00:02:01,840
SalesPlay recommends a plant-operations conversation to establish the
process and need.

28
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:05,670
It does not turn market growth into an assertion that Ridgeway is
buying.

29
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:09,600
The research should help Jamie act.

30
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:14,600
SalesPlay prepares a short opening, identifies the plant operations
team, and suggests three

31
00:02:14,970 --> 00:02:18,680
questions about the process, production need, and timing.

32
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:24,240
Jamie can edit the message. Editing clears its review, and saving
records the evidence version

33
00:02:24,610 --> 00:02:26,600
behind the play. Nothing is sent.

34
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:30,400
This keeps the agent’s work connected to a practical seller outcome.

35
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:34,970
The seller can inspect the evidence and judge the opening, without
having to run every research

36
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:39,490
task or understand a factory capacity model before the first
conversation.

37
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:43,300
Now record a sample buyer correction.

38
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:48,760
Ridgeway buys finished cores from a supplier, so assembly is not done in
its plant.

39
00:02:48,760 --> 00:02:51,720
The earlier equipment approach no longer fits.

40
00:02:51,955 --> 00:02:57,370
SalesPlay records that fact for Ridgeway, flags the saved play for
review, and changes its

41
00:02:57,370 --> 00:03:03,240
investigation. The next account must have explicit, recent evidence of
in-house assembly.

42
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:09,520
Watch the revised plan investigate Evergreen, a different manufacturer
outside Jamie’s account

43
00:03:09,890 --> 00:03:13,400
list. The seller has supplied one consequential fact.

44
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:16,640
The system changes the work it performs next.

45
00:03:16,640 --> 00:03:21,470
The original goal remains intact, and the earlier decision remains
visible in the history.

46
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:28,400
Evergreen has stronger process evidence, but a product-segment question
remains.

47
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:33,930
SalesPlay prepares an analyst handoff with the goal, the operations
profile, the segment map, and

48
00:03:33,930 --> 00:03:38,720
a precise question. No request is actually sent in this prototype.

49
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:41,540
Apply the clearly labeled sample analyst finding.

50
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:47,080
It keeps Evergreen within the segment map, while leaving the production
need unconfirmed.

51
00:03:47,080 --> 00:03:50,000
The next step becomes a buyer conversation.

52
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:55,430
If the buyer later confirms an assembly need, an application engineer
should assess technical fit.

53
00:03:55,490 --> 00:04:01,450
The proposed agent chooses the right validation route instead of
inventing an answer it cannot support.

54
00:04:02,880 --> 00:04:06,680
Open the research activity to see the goal and its decision history.

55
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:12,630
The Ridgeway correction, the revised search, the Evergreen evidence, and
the analyst finding stay

56
00:04:13,110 --> 00:04:17,870
connected. The previous Ridgeway play remains available as history, with
export blocked because

57
00:04:18,080 --> 00:04:23,970
its facts have changed. A sample monitoring update shows the next part
of the future experience.

58
00:04:24,330 --> 00:04:29,600
HarborGrid’s expansion review is postponed, so SalesPlay changes the
timing recommendation.

59
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:33,600
This is a manually played event; no live watcher is active.

60
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:39,310
The intended product would keep investigating within an agreed scope and
surface material changes

61
00:04:39,370 --> 00:04:41,670
that deserve the seller’s attention.

62
00:04:42,840 --> 00:04:48,410
The product bet is that MarketsandMarkets can make this investigation
more useful through granular

63
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:54,360
market relationships and analyst expertise. Research must improve the
account decision, rather

64
00:04:54,360 --> 00:05:00,640
than simply add more text. SalesPlay already markets agents, and
competitors also promise research

65
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:05,490
and execution. The differentiation to prove is the quality of the
research-to-decision loop.

66
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:09,320
Start with one product line, one territory, and five accounts.

67
00:05:09,660 --> 00:05:15,280
Compare public research with entitled specialist intelligence, holding
the workflow constant.

68
00:05:15,650 --> 00:05:21,040
Measure useful novelty, specificity, correctness, buyer-confirmed need,
and effort.

69
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:27,340
The manager can now evaluate a clear direction: the seller owns the
goal, agents investigate, and

70
00:05:27,420 --> 00:05:30,840
customer facts continuously steer the next useful action.

